What "Native Provider" Actually Means Here
Freedom Browser already has:

Injected provider (window.ethereum) in the webview, running in an isolated world

Main-process wallet management with pluggable Signer backends

IPC between the injected provider and main process

So we are not building a page-level provider. We are building a main-process signing backend that the existing injected provider talks to. This is still a significant security improvement over a typical injected-only wallet because:

The signing logic lives in the main process, not in the page

The page only sees the injected provider, which is controlled by the browser

The actual private key operations happen in Vaughan, outside the browser entirely

This is the correct architecture for an Electron-based browser.

