Critical Open Decision: signTransaction vs. eth_sendTransaction
The Signer contract in Freedom Browser expects signTransaction to return a serialized signed transaction (so the browser can broadcast via its own RPC pool). If our backend maps signTransaction to eth_sendTransaction, Vaughan would broadcast the transaction directly, breaking the contract.

Resolution: Add a new method to our Rust provider server:

vaughan_signTransaction – signs and returns the raw signed transaction without broadcasting

Keep eth_sendTransaction for cases where Vaughan handles the full broadcast (e.g., our own dApp launcher)

This is the correct approach. Your Vaughan agent should implement this method as part of FR-2.2.

What Vaughan's Provider Server Must Provide
Based on the browser integration, our Rust vaughan-provider server must support these methods (not all are currently defined):

Method	Purpose
eth_accounts	Return active account (empty if locked)
eth_requestAccounts	Connect/authorize, return account
eth_chainId	Return active chain ID
eth_sendTransaction	Sign and broadcast (for direct dApp use)
personal_sign	EIP-191 sign
eth_signTypedData_v4	EIP-712 sign
