We should also implement accountsChanged and chainChanged event broadcasting to connected clients, as the browser's injected provider will subscribe to these.

Revised Understanding of Security
Injected provider (in webview) is not under our control; it's the browser's own code. But because it runs in an isolated world and communicates with the main process via Electron IPC, it's protected from direct page manipulation.

Our backend runs in the main process and communicates with Vaughan over loopback WebSocket. It never exposes keys or signing logic to the page.

The overall security is therefore still stronger than a typical JavaScript-injected wallet, and aligns with the "native provider" goal.

